TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "player archetypes",
    "offensive roles",
    "cut actions",
    "drive actions",
    "role transferability",
    "player effectiveness across contexts"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "defining player archetypes based on play types",
    "quantifying player reliance on cuts vs. drives",
    "measuring changes in player effectiveness in new environments",
    "controlling for team and contextual factors in player role evaluation",
    "sample size considerations for player archetype studies"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No historical evidence was available to establish any findings regarding the transferability of cut-heavy vs. drive-heavy player roles."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The research question implies comparing changes in player effectiveness or efficiency after changing teams, for players categorized by their primary offensive action (cuts vs. drives).",
    "The available data allows for identifying play types (cuts, drives) and player initiators, as well as tracking player performance across seasons.",
    "The 'n=24' units available is critically small for robust statistical analysis, especially when comparing two groups and controlling for confounding factors."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The precise definition of 'shapers' is not provided, requiring a clear operationalization.",
    "The exact meaning of 'roles travel better' needs a quantitative definition (e.g., maintaining efficiency, improving, degrading less).",
    "The 'n=24' units is ambiguous; it is unclear if this refers to individual players, player-seasons, or player-team transitions, but in any case, it appears to be a very small sample for this type of comparison."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was available, so it did not change the decision."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.05,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "The stated 'n=24' units is severely insufficient to detect a meaningful difference between two player archetypes (cut-heavy vs. drive-heavy) in their role transferability, especially after accounting for confounding factors. This sample size makes it highly improbable to achieve useful statistical power."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "It is uncertain if there will be enough players who clearly fit into distinct 'cut-heavy' and 'drive-heavy' shaper categories AND who change teams within the available seasons to form a sufficient sample for comparison, even if the 'n=24' refers to transitions."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior work addressing this specific question was found in the historical records."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "The extremely low sample size (n=24) is the primary risk, making it nearly impossible to draw statistically significant conclusions or achieve adequate power for a meaningful comparison between two player archetypes."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Player effectiveness upon changing teams is highly confounded by factors such as the quality and scheme of the new team, the surrounding teammates, coaching changes, the player's age and development stage, and the specific reasons for the player's move (e.g., declining performance, strategic trade). Isolating the effect of 'role type' from these factors will be very challenging."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "It may be difficult to identify a sufficient number of players who consistently fit the 'cut-heavy shaper' or 'drive-heavy shaper' archetypes and also undergo team changes within the observed seasons to form a robust sample for analysis."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Defining 'cut-heavy' and 'drive-heavy' shapers consistently and reliably across different players and contexts may be challenging. The proportion of plays attributable to cuts or drives might fluctuate or be influenced by team schemes, making archetype classification difficult."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Players might be traded or signed after particularly good or bad seasons, leading to regression to the mean effects that could be misinterpreted as 'role travel' success or failure."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.05,
    "rejected": 0.05,
    "untestable": 0.7,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.1
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The difference in the mean change in offensive efficiency (e.g., adjusted points per possession or Offensive EPM) for players classified as 'cut-heavy shapers' versus 'drive-heavy shapers' after a team transition, compared to their performance on their previous team.",
    "unit": "Player-team transition. A 'unit' is defined as a player's performance in the season immediately following a team change, compared to their performance in the season immediately preceding the change.",
    "sample": "All players who changed teams between any two consecutive seasons within the 2022-23 to 2025-26 period. Players will be classified as 'cut-heavy shaper' or 'drive-heavy shaper' based on the proportion of their initiated possessions involving cuts or drives in the season prior to their team change (e.g., top quartile for one, bottom quartile for the other, with a minimum threshold of total initiated possessions). Given the n=24 constraint, this sample is likely too small to yield meaningful results.",
    "power": "Not estimable with the given n=24, but expected to be extremely low. A power analysis for a two-sample t-test with a small effect size (Cohen's d = 0.5) would require at least 128 observations per group for 80% power at alpha=0.05. With n=24 total units, achieving useful power is impossible.",
    "controls": "To mitigate confounding, potential controls include: player age, total minutes played, team offensive rating (prior and post transition), change in surrounding talent (e.g., average EPM of teammates), and coaching changes. However, with n=24, controlling for multiple variables is not feasible without exhausting degrees of freedom.",
    "falsification": "A falsification test could involve comparing the 'travel' success of players whose primary role type is not expected to have differential transferability, or examining if the observed effects persist after accounting for known confounders (if sufficient data existed).",
    "multiplicity": "No multiple testing correction planned due to the single primary hypothesis and extremely limited sample size, but this would be a consideration with a larger study.",
    "decision_rule": "Reject the null hypothesis (no difference in role transferability) if the p-value of the primary estimand is less than 0.05. However, due to severe power limitations, any non-significant result would be inconclusive.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 regular season and playoffs; NBA 2023-24 regular season and playoffs; NBA 2024-25 regular season and playoffs; NBA 2025-26 regular season and playoffs (complete as of 2026-06-21)."
  }
}

